  "language": "en",
  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "Your phone or fitness band can count steps with a tiny chip called an accelerometer. Inside this accelerometer, a microscopic proof mass shifts slightly when your body moves. Tiny flexible springs hold the mass inside the chip while allowing that small movement. As the mass shifts, the spacing between interlocking capacitor fingers changes. The accelerometer's electronics convert that spacing change into an acceleration signal. During walking, the signal contains a repeating pattern from each step and other body motion. Software filters noise and looks for the rhythm and strength of genuine steps rather than counting every bump. Each accepted step pattern advances the step counter by one. So your step count begins with motion inside the accelerometer and ends with software recognizing a walking pattern.",
  "mean_word_probability": 0.9727773435115814,
  "low_confidence_words": [],
